hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  one interesting dictum of Epicurus is  moral theory that we find running  throughout his works almost  word-for-word although sometimes  translated a little bit differently in  the english runs one cannot live  pleasantly without live incredibly  honorably and justly now that's a very  interesting thing to say for its own  sake and this is really what Epicurus  thinks in terms of the conditions for  the good life the life of happiness the  life that is actually as pleasant as  possible so what is there this  connection between pleasantness or  pleasure and living prudently living  honorably or we'll talk about exactly  what that means in a moment and living  justly he gives us some explanation of  two of these he doesn't give us much  explanation of the middle of one so what  we want to do is look at what each of  these terms actually means not just  lexically in terms of the greek and how  we would translate it but what it means  for Epicurus within his his moral theory  within his ethical system and then we  want to think about how these would  conduce to actually living  Pleasant life and now when he's talking  about living a pleasant life he doesn't  just mean one moment you might actually  translate this better as a pleasant  lifestyle a you know sustained process  of living that goes on and is structured  in a certain way there's certain ways of  living according to Epicurus that may in  fact that contains some pleasures or  pursue some pleasures but will not be  there for a pleasant life in any sort of  full sense so let's look first at this  discussion about living prudently the  greek term for that is fro nemo's and we  often translate phronesis as prudence or  practical wisdom it's an understanding  of what is actually good for human  beings what is bad for human beings how  to you know go about attaining our ends  how to you know do the right things in  situations not just right and a sort of  you know sense of fulfilling our duties  but right for us as well that's going to  conduce to our our living well and  enjoying ourselves for Epicure so from  your mouth you know living fro me most  is living according to practical wisdom  understanding the natures of pleasures  and pains is going to be of the essence  of that for Epicurus understanding  what's really valuable in life and what  is just based on what he calls  groundless or empty or vain opinions  that's all going to be part of phronesis  for him he actually says in the letter  to men osseous that the beginning and  greatest good for human beings is in  fact prudence prudence is a more  precious thing than even philosophy from  it Springs all the other virtues and  prudence is what actually teaches us  this dictum so this is not only about  prudence this is actually stemming from  prudence we'll look at that we know why  that's so important in a moment this  other term kalos in in greek  there's this this moral value sometimes  we translate it as beauty or fineness or  honorable Ness or you know it's sort of  intrinsic value and it's difficult to  get across it means different things in  different situations but Kalos in the  the adjective sense to live according to  that sort of standard to live a the  light of Kalos means to live a life well  to live a life you might say in a  certain graceful way in a way that's  appropriate in a way that makes sense in  a way that is adapted to what you know  is is finest for human beings the  opposite of that would be an ugly life a  life that is full of intrinsic badness a  life that's in many respects damaged or  it you know not fulfilling its its its  goals or its ends you know a life that  is stunted so he doesn't really tell us  much more about this in the writings  that we have preserved by him but you  know we could probably extrapolate some  things from other passages he will tell  us quite a bit about justice in fact so  much that I'm creating another core  concept video specifically on Epicurus  as discussions and conception of justice  but he says that we have to live justly  that is de caios in accordance with the  norms of justice now this is going to  include for him living according to you  might say a certain basic morality that  has to hold among all human beings if  we're going to live together in any sort  of harmonious way it also means  following the rules of the particular  society with with with within which one  lives provided those rules don't  actually go against these more  overarching norms of justice and  provided those rules are oriented toward  some sort of what he calls mutual  usefulness that is they they actually  benefit the people that are involved  with them so it totally  just regime one would not be living  justly by living in accordance with its  norms as a matter of fact you could be  living justly precisely by contesting  those norms but for the most part in  most societies and within the structure  of most relationships so within groups  because relationships are going to be  very important for for Epicurus there  are certain you might say basic  standards that we have to follow he also  says in the sovereign in the principle  doctrines that whenever one of these is  lacking the living prudently living  honorably or well living justly it's  impossible to live a pleasant life one  one could be for example living wisely  or living well and justly but not living  wisely and it wouldn't be a fully  Pleasant life so why is that the case  why are all three of these necessary why  do they in a certain respect call upon  and interpenetrate each other again I  think that will probably get more  mileage out of looking at the role of  prudence and justice than them thinking  about this particular standard because  Epicurus doesn't tell us that much about  it why can't you live a pleasant life  without living crudely  now what would it mean to live prudently  like we put here it mean to live in  accordance with practical wisdom does  that mean that you actually have to be  yourself a prudent person not  necessarily you could be guiding  yourself say during the years of  adolescence under the guidance the  tutelage of somebody else who's got more  you know to offer in that respect  somebody who's got more experience  somebody who actually has cultivated  practical wisdom hopefully you would  acquire it in turn but perhaps if you  had the right kind of friends and the  right kind of environment you wouldn't  necessarily need to develop prudence for  yourself  still it probably would be better if you  don't have prudence to rely upon well  you're going to start making dumb  decisions  short-sighted decisions  pleasures and pains if you don't fully  understand what truly is pleasant and  what just appears to be pleasant for the  the short-term but is actually going to  lead to greater pains or why certain  pains actually ought to be endured in  the short term so that we can have  greater pleasure in the long term if you  don't have that sort of orientation  you're going to make bad decisions which  are going to lead to you not actually  living pleasantly and probably not you  know honorably probably not even justly  also if you live a life that is lacking  in justice if you decide well I'm going  to cheat I'm going to lie I'm going to  steal I'm going to subvert the norms  Epicurus actually thinks you can never  be totally sure that you're going to get  away with it you might actually be  bothered by the fact that you're doing  that sort of stuff you know you maybe  you feel pangs of conscience that would  be pains that would be lack of pleasure  but he thinks that those who do wrong in  general you have to live in a state of  fear so if you don't live justly then  you're going to live unpleasantly it's  an interesting question could you live  prudently without living justly or could  you live honorably without living justly  I think Epicurus would probably say no  in those cases that's not really going  to to be sustainable maybe in the short  term yes but not not in the long term so  all three of these are required as not  only conditions for the pleasant life  you might say a certain set of  components or dimensions of the pleasant  life but they're also what's productive  of the pleasant life acting justly  allows you to live in a matrix of  relationships with human beings who are  going to think that you're a jerk and  won't distrust you and won't want to  punish you when they find out the sort  of bad things that you've done right  acting prudently will permit you to  decide you know what things to pursue  and what things to avoid and they will  interface you might say your intersect  with each other in a life that is truly  Pleasant and Epicurus thinks that that's  going to be a life  the virtues where these are you might  say ways of living virtuously
 

